      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg/day. That is twice the maximum labeled daily dose of 3,200 mg/day for adults (OTC labeling is 1,200 mg/day). At that dose the risks of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events go up a lot, and the higher dose gives no added analgesic or anti-inflammatory benefit.\n\nHere are some options within the labeled limits:\n- **800 mg PO four times daily** (3,200 mg/day), the maximum labeled dose.\n- **600 mg PO four times daily** (2,400 mg/day), a common dose for inflammatory pain.\n- **400 mg PO every 6 hours** (1,600 mg/day) for mild to moderate pain.\n\nMaybe the intended dose was **1600 mg/day**, for example 400 mg four times daily, or **800 mg** per dose. If so, tell me which one and I'll enter it. If the patient needs more analgesia than the maximum ibuprofen dose gives, I can also enter an adjunct such as scheduled acetaminophen, up to 3\u20134 g/day."
      }
     ],
